# How to Use This Document

## What this document is

This is not a textbook of Gender Studies. It teaches only as much of the subject as you need to find your way. It is the **map** you study before you study, and the map you come back to in the last fortnight, when you must decide what to revise.

It does six jobs:

1. It starts you from zero. Part Zero explains what Gender Studies is, the twelve ideas that unlock the whole subject, about a hundred words you must know, the thinkers you will meet, and the timeline behind the paper. It assumes you have never opened a book on the subject.
2. It explains the paper. What the syllabus says, which books help, how the paper is printed, how much time each question gets, and how marks are actually earned.
3. It dissects the record. Every descriptive question from CE-2016 to CE-2026 (==77 questions==, which become ==84 items== once the short notes are split) and all ==140 recovered MCQs== have been tagged by topic, by question family and by command word.
4. It rebuilds the syllabus. The nine syllabus heads are uneven. They are rebuilt into ==seven topics==, each a unit you can master in two to three weeks, and each weighted by how often the examiner visits it.
5. It sets the method. How a Gender Studies answer is built: the answer map, headings taken from the question's own words, evidence inside every heading, diagrams, the Pakistan angle, the balanced view and the conclusion.
6. It gives you a plan. A sixteen-week timetable, a memory routine for the facts, and a minute-by-minute drill for the day of the paper.

## Who it is written for

It is written for a candidate who has **never studied Gender Studies**. Perhaps you chose it because it is short, because it is useful for Essay and Current Affairs, or because a friend scored well in it. You may not know what "feminism" really means, what "patriarchy" is, or why anyone would say that "gender is socially constructed". That is fine. Every document in this set starts where you are.

- Every technical term is explained the first time it appears, and again in the word tables of Part Zero.
- Key terms you must be able to define are printed in bold green, like **gender**. Dates and numbers you must memorise are printed in bold navy, like ==1972==.
- The English is deliberately simple: short sentences, one idea in each. Where a thinker's own words are worth quoting in the exam, they are given exactly and then explained.
- Every idea is tied to an example, and wherever possible to an example from Pakistan.

::: simple
Think of this subject as a pair of glasses. Once you put them on, you start to notice that the same event — a flood, a budget, an election, a school timetable — affects women and men differently. Gender Studies is the discipline that explains why, and asks what can be done about it.
:::

## The family of documents

This Master Anatomy is the first document of the set. Each of the seven topics then gets two Word documents. When all seven are finished, five volumes are compiled from them.

@py family_docs

::: table | The documents of the set and what each one does.
@widths 1.6,4,8.4
Code | Document | What it does
MA | The Master Anatomy | This document: the subject from zero, the paper, the record, the seven topics, the method and the plan.
TnKB | Topic Knowledge Base (T1KB … T7KB) | Everything you need for the topic, taught from zero in simple English, ==15,000–19,000 words== each: key terms, chapters built from the syllabus lines and the examiner's questions, evidence cards inside every point, 10–18 diagrams, a chapter of contemporary debates, the topic's past questions and five likely questions, a one-liner bank and a revision sheet.
TnQA | Topic Question Answers (T1QA … T7QA) | Every past question of the topic answered as a model of ==1,000–1,300 words==: the answer map, a decoding of the question, headings from the question's own words, evidence in every heading, a diagram, the Pakistan angle and a conclusion.
FB | The Fact Book | Every fact, date, statistic, law, report, thinker, book, conference and case used anywhere in the set, arranged topic by topic and kept up to date — the book you memorise from.
QA | The Question Answers print volume | All seven TnQA documents in one volume, in syllabus order.
OL | The One-Liner and MCQ Bank | The one-liners of every KB, all 140 past MCQs with explained keys, and new practice MCQs on every syllabus line.
RN | The Revision Notes | The whole subject for the last three weeks — fuller than a summary: every topic's arguments, theories, facts and answer frames, in about ==4,500 words per topic==.
PR | The Prediction Papers (Sets 1–3) | Three complete papers set exactly as FPSC prints them — 20 MCQs and 7 descriptive questions each — with keys and full model answers.
:::

## How the pages are built

Every document in the set uses the same page furniture, so you always know what you are looking at. The most important piece is the **evidence card**. Evidence is what separates a 12-mark answer from a 16-mark one, and in this set evidence always sits *inside* the point it proves, never in a separate list at the end. There are seven kinds of card:

::: define Gender | the core idea of the whole subject
The roles, behaviours, activities and attributes that a society considers appropriate for women and men. Gender is learnt, it changes over time, and it differs from one society to another.
-> A teal card is a definition you should be able to reproduce in the exam, almost word for word.
:::

::: thinker Simone de Beauvoir | French philosopher · The Second Sex (1949)
"One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman."
-> A plum card is a thinker's idea. When the exact words are well known they are quoted; when they are not, the idea is summarised in plain English — never invented.
:::

::: data Global Gender Gap Report 2026 | World Economic Forum
Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== economies, with ==59.5%== of its gender gap closed. In 2025 it had ranked last, ==148th of 148==.
-> A blue card is data: a number, its source and its year. Data dates quickly, so every data card names its year, and the Fact Book is updated before each exam.
:::

::: law Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the Name or Pretext of Honour) Act 2016 | Pakistan
Makes the punishment of life imprisonment compulsory in honour killings, even if the victim's family forgives the killer.
-> A navy card is a law, a policy or an international convention, with its year and its key provision.
:::

The other three cards are **QUOTATION** (a famous line from a report, a slogan or a leader), **CASE** (a real event or person, usually from Pakistan) and **REPORT** (the finding of an institution such as UN Women, HRCP or the World Bank).

Around the cards you will meet a set of boxes, each with one job:

::: table | The boxes and what each one means.
@widths 3,11
Box | What it means
IN SIMPLE WORDS | A hard idea retold as if to a friend. Read it first if the paragraph above it confused you.
EXAMPLE | The idea seen in real life — in a family, a village, an office, a law or a news story.
TRAP | A mistake candidates commonly make — a wrong date, a confused term, an argument that annoys examiners.
EXAMINER'S EYE | What the examiner is looking for in this question or topic, and how to give it to him or her.
HOT DEBATE | A live controversy — transgender rights, Aurat March, NGO-isation, climate, digital violence — set out fairly.
BALANCED VIEW | The main positions on a sensitive question, stated fairly, with the line a civil-service candidate should take.
PAKISTAN ANGLE | The Constitution, the laws, the institutions and the data that make an answer applied rather than abstract.
REMEMBER | A fact, list or line worth memorising word for word.
LATEST UPDATE | Something that changed in 2025–2026 which older books and notes do not have.
:::

## The rules these notes keep

Gender Studies deals with real people's lives, with law and with faith. You never know who your examiner is: a professor of sociology, a women's-rights activist, a retired civil servant or a religious scholar. Every document in this set therefore keeps six rules.

1. **Accuracy first.** Every date, number, law and quotation is checked. Where a number comes from a survey, the survey and its year are named. Where sources disagree, the notes say so.
2. **Nothing invented.** No quotation is put into anyone's mouth. No statistic is rounded into something it is not. Weak or doubtful "facts" that circulate in guidebooks are flagged, not repeated.
3. **Balance.** On contested questions — religion and women's rights, the Aurat March, the transgender law, Western versus local feminism — the notes present each side fairly and then give the reasoned, constitutional position a future civil servant can defend in any room.
4. **The Pakistan angle everywhere.** Every topic is applied to Pakistan: the Constitution of ==1973==, the laws, the institutions, the data and the cases. The examiner rewards application far more than theory recited from a Western textbook.
5. **Up to date.** The facts are current to September 2026 (for example, the Global Gender Gap Report 2026, the Labour Force Survey 2024–25, the Islamabad child-marriage law of 2025). The Fact Book carries the latest figures and is the place to refresh them before your exam.
6. **Simple English.** Short sentences. One idea per sentence. Every term explained. If a sentence needs to be read twice, it has been rewritten.

::: eye | Why balance scores
An answer that shouts — at the West, at religion, at men, at "feminists" — loses marks with every kind of examiner. An answer that defines its terms, presents evidence, admits the strongest counter-argument and then reaches a reasoned conclusion wins marks with all of them. Balance is not a lack of opinion. It is an opinion that has earned the right to be heard.
:::

## The reading order

1. Read **Part Zero** slowly. It is Gender Studies from zero: the ideas, the vocabulary, the thinkers and the whole syllabus in twenty minutes. Come back to its word tables whenever a term puzzles you.
2. Read **Parts One to Three** once, quickly. They explain the paper and the record. You do not need to memorise them.
3. Read a topic's **dossier in Part Four** just before you begin that topic's Knowledge Base.
4. Read **Part Five** (method) before you write your first practice answer, and again before your first prediction paper.
5. Return to **Part Three** and **Part Six** in the last fortnight to decide what to revise and how to spend the day of the paper.
